@@ -4,6 +4,10 @@
 Kilonovae are high-energy astrophysical transients formed by the merger of a two neutron stars. They are short-lived (a few weeks) and evolve rapidly (i.e. drop in brightness), with the SED quickly shifting to the redder wavelengths in a matter of days after the merger. Which is why it is important to follow-up kilonovae ASAP, and get spectra across visible and NIR bands.
 
 ZShooter will be uniquely suited with its rapid ToO capability, to observe KNe early and late in the sequence.
+
+The detector package and independent exposure counts are explicit below. Calibrations and science products are separated by detector mode. Both raw stochastic frames and accumulated electron/variance inputs are saved for reduction.
+
+**Runtime repair (2026-10-20):** all three epochs now extract in all six channels. BLUE's native iteration allowance is 4,000; the preserved failing 20-day swath converges at iteration 3,304 with the unchanged tolerance. The flat-AB controls, epoch/channel plots and host-component checks have been rerun. Science reductions reused the saved stochastic frames and calibration geometry. See [the runtime assessment](../docs/notebook-runtime-checks.md) for the checks and [the operating-point assessment](../docs/detector-operating-points.md#reduction-and-verification) for the separate requested-MJD forwarding issue in these saved frames.
 
